plating solutions, dry film photoresists, and laminates and polyimide films. The company was formerly known as Novus SpinCo 1, Inc. and changed its name to Qnity Electronics, Inc. in April 2025. The company was incorporated in 2024 and is headquartered in Wilmington, Delaware.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Jon D. Kemp | CEO & Director | Age 48 | Compensation: $2.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 10K people. Revenue per employee of $475K ranks in the above the sector median among Technology peers. Each employee generates $99K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $2K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (4/10).","financial_summary":["Qnity Electronics, Inc. is a large-cap technology business with a $24.7B market cap on $4.8B of annual revenue.","Returns on capital run at 8% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $988M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 21 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.84\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 2.2\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage of 16\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Goodwill represents 53% of total assets \u2014 a large impairment risk hanging over book value","sev":"red","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Priced near or above es